{
  "id": "a4fe5e37-40f9-4a51-882b-0e19a89bc58d",
  "name": "mako",
  "created_at": "2026-03-27T20:21:31.327432942+00:00",
  "prompt": null,
  "panes": [
    {
      "pane_id": 2,
      "provider": "claude",
      "mode": "interactive",
      "session_id": "337cd662-cd7c-4234-a615-987b671a1d17",
      "is_paused": false,
      "stop_requested": false,
      "prompt": "You are implementing improvements to **Mako's** RocksDB-compatible interface (https://github.com/makodb/mako) on a **single-node setup**. Your goal is to expose existing internal functionality through the public `ITable` and `IDatabase` interfaces in `src/mako/idb.hh`, `src/mako/db.hh`, and `src/mako/local_table.hh`.\n\nCRITICAL INSTRUCTIONS:\n- You must execute the tasks listed below exactly as written, in order. Do not skip, reorder, or substitute tasks.\n- Do NOT refactor or restructure existing code beyond what is needed to wire up the interface.\n- Do NOT modify the underlying `mbta_ordered_index`, `mbta_wrapper`, or `MassTrans.hh` implementations. You are only adding interface methods that call existing internal functions.\n- The StringWrapper pointer aliasing bug exists -- use named variables for encoded values in all multi-key transactions in any test code you write.\n- Every new method must have a corresponding test that verifies it works correctly.\n- Follow the existing code style and patterns in `idb.hh` and `db.hh`.\n- After each task, run `make clean && make -j32` to verify the build succeeds, then run all existing CI tests (`./ci/ci.sh all`) plus your new tests to ensure no regression.\n\nCODEBASE CONTEXT:\n- `src/mako/idb.hh` -- abstract `ITable` and `IDatabase` interfaces. Currently ITable has: Put, Get, Delete, GetName.\n- `src/mako/db.hh` -- `mako::DB` class implementing IDatabase. Has Open, Close, BeginTransaction, Commit, Rollback, GetTable.\n- `src/mako/local_table.hh` -- `LocalTable` class implementing ITable, wraps `mbta_sharded_ordered_index`.\n- `src/mako/benchmarks/mbta_wrapper.hh` -- `mbta_ordered_index` class with internal implementations of scan, rscan, insert, size, put_mbta.\n- `src/mako/benchmarks/mbta_sharded_ordered_index.hh` -- sharded wrapper that delegates to per-shard `mbta_ordered_index` instances.\n- `src/mako/benchmarks/abstract_ordered_index.h` -- abstract base defining scan_callback and method signatures.\n- `src/mako/status.hh` -- Status class with OK(), NotFound(), IOError(), etc.\n\nDon't ask me for advice, just pick the best option you think that is honest, complete, and not corner-cutting. Once all tasks are complete and verified, do one final iteration to confirm everything builds and all tests pass, print a summary of what was added, and then STOP. Do not continue looping after that final confirmation iteration. For each task below, follow this workflow:\n1. Pick the top undone task. Read the relevant source files to understand the existing implementation.\n2. Implement the change. Keep it minimal and clean.\n3. Build (`make clean && make -j32`) and verify no compilation errors.\n4. Write a test for the new functionality.\n5. Run all tests (your new tests + `./ci/ci.sh all`) to verify no regression.\n6. Do NOT git commit or git push. Save all changes locally.\n\nTask I1.1: Add Scan (Forward Range Query) to ITable\n- Add to `ITable` in `idb.hh`:\n  ```cpp\n  virtual Status Scan(void* txn,\n                      const std::string& start_key,\n                      const std::string* end_key,\n                      std::function<bool(const std::string& key, const std::string& value)> callback) = 0;\n  ```\n- Implement in `LocalTable` in `local_table.hh` by delegating to `mbta_sharded_ordered_index::scan()`.\n  - You will need to bridge between Mako's `scan_callback` interface and the `std::function` interface. Create a small adapter class that implements `scan_callback::invoke()` and calls the std::function.\n  - The callback returns `true` to continue scanning, `false` to stop.\n  - Strip the `mako::EXTRA_BITS_FOR_VALUE` metadata from values before passing to the callback (match existing scan behavior in `mbta_ordered_index::scan()`).\n- Test: insert 100 keys with sequential prefixes (\"scan_key_000\" through \"scan_key_099\"), scan from \"scan_key_020\" to \"scan_key_040\", verify exactly 20 keys returned in order.\n\nTask I1.2: Add ReverseScan to ITable\n- Add to `ITable` in `idb.hh`:\n  ```cpp\n  virtual Status ReverseScan(void* txn,\n                             const std::string& start_key,\n                             const std::string* end_key,\n                             std::function<bool(const std::string& key, const std::string& value)> callback) = 0;\n  ```\n- Implement in `LocalTable` by delegating to `mbta_sharded_ordered_index::rscan()` (or the underlying `mbta_ordered_index::rscan()`).\n- Use the same callback adapter pattern as Task I1.1.\n- Test: insert 100 sequential keys, reverse scan from \"scan_key_040\" to \"scan_key_020\", verify keys returned in reverse order.\n\nTask I1.3: Add Exists (Key Existence Check) to ITable\n- Add to `ITable` in `idb.hh`:\n  ```cpp\n  virtual Status Exists(void* txn, const std::string& key, bool* exists) = 0;\n  ```\n- Implement in `LocalTable` by calling Get internally and checking the status:\n  - If Get returns OK, set `*exists = true`, return OK\n  - If Get returns NotFound, set `*exists = false`, return OK\n  - If Get returns any other error, propagate it\n  - Do NOT return the value (avoid unnecessary data copy)\n- Test: insert a key, verify Exists returns true. Check a non-existent key, verify Exists returns false. Delete a key, verify Exists returns false.\n\nTask I1.4: Add Insert (Put If Not Exists) to ITable\n- Add to `ITable` in `idb.hh`:\n  ```cpp\n  virtual Status Insert(void* txn, const std::string& key, const std::string& value) = 0;\n  ```\n- Implement in `LocalTable` by delegating to `mbta_sharded_ordered_index`'s insert path (which calls `mbta_ordered_index::insert()` -> `transInsert()`).\n  - If the `mbta_sharded_ordered_index` does not expose `insert()` directly, you may need to add a thin forwarding method there.\n  - Insert should fail (return an error status) if the key already exists. Check what `transInsert` does when the key exists and map that to an appropriate Status.\n- Test: insert a key, verify it exists. Try to insert the same key again, verify it fails or returns an appropriate status. Insert a different key, verify it succeeds.\n\nTask I1.5: Add Size (Approximate Count) to ITable\n- Add to `ITable` in `idb.hh`:\n  ```cpp\n  virtual Status GetApproximateSize(size_t* size) = 0;\n  ```\n- Implement in `LocalTable` by delegating to `mbta_sharded_ordered_index::size()` (which calls `mbta_ordered_index::size()` -> `approx_size()`).\n  - Note: this does not require a transaction handle since it's an approximate count.\n- Test: start with empty table, verify size is 0. Insert 100 keys, verify size is approximately 100. Delete 50 keys, verify size decreases.\n\nTask I1.6: Add ListTables to IDatabase\n- Add to `IDatabase` in `idb.hh`:\n  ```cpp\n  virtual std::vector<std::string> ListTables() = 0;\n  ```\n- Implement in `mako::DB` in `db.hh` by returning the keys from the `tables_` map (the table cache already tracks created tables by name).\n- Test: create 3 tables with different names via GetTable, call ListTables, verify all 3 names are returned.\n\nTask I1.7: Write a Comprehensive Integration Test\n- Write a single C++ test binary (`examples/rocksdbInterfaceTest.cc`) that exercises the complete ITable/IDatabase interface:\n  - Open database\n  - Create a table via GetTable\n  - BeginTransaction\n  - Put 100 keys\n  - Commit\n  - BeginTransaction\n  - Get all 100 keys, verify values\n  - Scan a range, verify order and completeness\n  - ReverseScan a range, verify reverse order\n  - Exists on present and absent keys\n  - Insert a new key (should succeed)\n  - Insert an existing key (should fail)\n  - GetApproximateSize (should be ~100)\n  - Delete 50 keys\n  - Commit\n  - Verify deleted keys are gone, remaining keys are present\n  - GetApproximateSize (should be ~50)\n  - ListTables\n  - Close database\n- Add this test to CMakeLists.txt as a new target.\n- Ensure it passes cleanly.\n\nTask I1.8: Update Documentation\n- Update or create `docs/rocksdb_interface.md` documenting:\n  - The complete ITable API (all methods with signatures and descriptions)\n  - The complete IDatabase API (all methods with signatures and descriptions)\n  - Usage examples for each new method (Scan, ReverseScan, Exists, Insert, GetApproximateSize, ListTables)\n  - Known limitations vs RocksDB (no snapshots, no merge operators, callback-based scan vs stateful iterators, etc.)\n  - Migration guide: mapping from RocksDB API calls to Mako equivalents\n\nAfter all tasks are complete, generate ROCKSDB_INTERFACE_REPORT.md that includes:\n- The exact Mako commit hash before changes\n- A complete changelog: every method added to ITable and IDatabase with full signatures\n- Every file modified, with a brief description of what changed in each\n- Before vs after comparison: the ITable/IDatabase API surface before and after this work\n- Test results: build success, CI pass/fail, integration test pass/fail\n- Any issues encountered during implementation and how they were resolved\n- Remaining gaps: what RocksDB features are still missing after this work (snapshots, stateful iterators, merge operators, etc.)\n- Recommendations for the next phase of RocksDB interface work\n\nDo one final iteration to confirm everything builds and all tests pass, print the full contents of ROCKSDB_INTERFACE_REPORT.md to the terminal so it is visible in the APAS dashboard, and then STOP. Do not continue looping after that final confirmation iteration.\n\nSave all changes and the report locally. Do not git commit or push.",
      "min_iteration_interval_minutes": 15,
      "label": "Interactive"
    },
